session:pr690Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #670 was filed as a follow-up during #475/PR #671: the --data-dir changelog only ever reconstructed the default \"ds\" dataset at startup. Reading persistence.rs and every changelog-writing call site (graph_store.rs, sparql_update.rs, upload.rs, ottr_endpoint.rs, rml_endpoint.rs, query.rs, admin.rs) revealed this was worse than the issue described: LogEntry's quad-mutation variants (ClearGraph/InsertQuad/DeleteQuad) carried NO dataset field at all, so writes to a non-\"ds\" dataset were durably logged into the single shared redb table but silently replayed into \"ds\" on restart -- active mislabeling, not just non-restoration. An advisor consult before implementation caught two design gaps the initial plan missed: (1) admin_create_dataset/admin_delete_dataset never touched the changelog at all, so an empty created dataset couldn't survive a restart and a deleted one had no way to stay deleted -- fixed by adding LogEntry::CreateDataset/DeleteDataset markers, with each name's most recent CreateDataset acting as a replay cutoff so a delete-then-recreate cycle starts empty rather than resurrecting quads or rulesets from the deleted incarnation; (2) admin_compact rewrote the whole changelog table from a single Datastore (always \"ds\"), silently destroying every other dataset's history on every compaction -- fixed by compact_multi, which snapshots every dataset in the registry under one registry-then-store-then-changelog lock order. The `dataset` field on quad-mutation LogEntry variants uses #[serde(default = \"ds\")] rather than a schema migration, since every historical on-disk entry genuinely was for \"ds\" (no prior version of the changelog ever supported another dataset) -- verified by a dedicated unit test that hand-writes an old-format JSON row with no dataset field and confirms it still replays correctly. A mechanical first pass threaded AppState.dataset_name through every LogEntry-constructing call site and stamped `state.dataset_name.clone()` everywhere uniformly; building and testing caught that this was wrong for dataset_ottr_post and dataset_rml_post specifically, since those two handlers are wired directly as /{name}/ottr and /{name}/rml route handlers in server.rs (not routed through dataset_routes::dataset_state() like every other per-dataset route), so `state` there is always the root/\"ds\" AppState regardless of which dataset the request targets -- fixed to stamp from the `name` path param instead. TDD: sparql_endpoint/tests/persist_multi_dataset.rs (7 tests, written and ignored first) plus 8 new persistence.rs unit tests, all green on first implementation pass; all pre-existing persistence/ruleset/fuseki_compat/graph_store/ottr/rml test suites re-run and confirmed unaffected. cargo fmt/clippy/test --workspace and cargo check --workspace --all-targets all clean; full CI (including the previously-flagged Security Audit job from #678, evidently resolved on main since) came back green." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:690 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr690Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr690 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr690Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Kept the quad-mutation and ruleset-mutation log entries for every dataset in the SAME redb table/sequence the changelog already used for \"ds\", tagging each entry with a `dataset` field, rather than giving each dataset its own table or its own redb file." ;
    agp:alternative "A per-dataset table (or per-dataset changelog file) was considered, since it would make compact_multi and replay_dataset simpler (no need to filter a shared iterator by dataset name). Rejected because it would break the append-order guarantee the existing ruleset-replay logic depends on: ReplaceRuleset/SetNamedRuleset/DeleteNamedRuleset entries for the SAME dataset must replay in their original relative order (a later SetNamedRuleset for the same ruleset-id must win over an earlier one), and CreateDataset/DeleteDataset markers need a single global order relative to a dataset's own quad and ruleset entries to make the delete-then-recreate cutoff logic well-defined. A single shared, globally-ordered sequence keeps that invariant free; splitting into per-dataset logs would require an explicit cross-log ordering mechanism (e.g. a global logical clock) to reconstruct the same guarantee, which is strictly more complexity for a server this codebase's own docs describe as memory-constrained and not needing to shard writes." .
